&lt;h2&gt;How We Compared Profound and Semrush&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We evaluated both tools on six criteria: engine coverage, prompt intelligence, technical and crawler analytics, dashboards and workflows, pricing, and team fit. Vendor-published pricing pages, product documentation, and a research compilation of each tool’s features supplied the data. Where a claim comes from a vendor, we say so. Where it’s editorial judgment, we say that too.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Disclosure:&lt;/strong&gt; HubSpot makes HubSpot AEO, which appears later in this article. We placed it after the neutral comparison so you can weigh Profound and Semrush on their own terms first.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;One more note on signals. Visibility in AI answers, citations, referral traffic, and deals are four different measurements. Improving one doesn’t prove causal revenue impact, and no tool here offers complete revenue attribution.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What is the core difference between Profound and Semrush for AEO?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AEO, or answer engine optimization, is the practice of earning accurate, favorable mentions and citations in AI-generated answers. SEO targets visibility in traditional results. The two share plenty of tactics, though they differ in measurement, tooling, and what winning means. Many teams treat AEO as part of a broader &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/generative-engine-optimization"&gt;generative engine optimization strategy&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Profound is a specialist AEO platform. It was built for answer engine optimization, server-log crawler analytics, SKU-level AI shopping data, and no-code content automation. Semrush offers AEO visibility features inside a broader SEO suite, tying AI visibility to its keyword database, technical site audits, and backlink profiles.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;That split shapes four things you’ll feel in daily use:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Data depth.&lt;/strong&gt; A specialist can go deeper on one problem. Profound’s server-log analysis and demographic prompt data have no direct counterpart in Semrush’s AEO toolkit.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Roadmap.&lt;/strong&gt; Profound’s roadmap centers on answer engines. Semrush’s spreads across SEO, social, advertising, local, and PR, so AEO shares attention with those priorities.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Workflow ownership.&lt;/strong&gt; With Semrush, your SEO team can own AEO inside a tool it already knows. With Profound, someone owns a new system.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Stack complexity.&lt;/strong&gt; The specialist adds a tool. The suite consolidates one.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The shift is worth acting on. Pew Research Center found that &lt;a href="https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2025/07/22/google-users-are-less-likely-to-click-on-links/"&gt;users clicked a traditional result link in 8% of visits when a Google AI summary appeared, versus 15% of visits without one&lt;/a&gt;. Gartner &lt;a href="https://www.gartner.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2024-02-19-gartner-predicts-search-engine-volume-will-drop-25-percent-by-2026-due-to-ai-chatbots-and-other-virtual-agents"&gt;predicted that traditional search engine volume would drop 25% by 2026&lt;/a&gt; as AI chatbots and virtual agents take over queries, though that figure is a forecast, not a measured result.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Profound vs.&amp;nbsp;Semrush AEO At a Glance&lt;/h3&gt; 
